  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR Reducing Alcohol Related Harms in India-Policy, Practice and Sustainable Development</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Problem drinking causes substantial harm to individuals, families, and societies worldwide, contributing to violence, lost productivity, and increased healthcare and law enforcement costs, with the burden falling disproportionately on poorer communities as alcohol use rises in countries such as India. Despite the scale of the problem, only a small proportion of people with drinking problems in India can access appropriate care due to health systems struggling to integrate and scale effective treatments. This programme seeks to bridge the gap between evidence and large-scale implementation by developing and testing contextually relevant, scalable psychosocial interventions for problem drinking and depression in low-resource settings, including approaches that engage families and communities to improve treatment uptake and outcomes. Alongside evaluating these interventions in real-world settings, the programme will examine strategies for sustainably embedding them within routine healthcare systems through active collaboration with service users, families, clinicians, community leaders, and policymakers. The work aims not only to improve outcomes for participants in the short term, but also to generate practical guidance on how effective mental health interventions can be sustainably scaled and made accessible to underserved populations in India and similar low-resource contexts globally.</narrative>
  </description>
